<h1>Mike Quigley</h1>
<p>Candidate for Mayor, Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027</p>
<p><a href="https://www.quigleyforchicago.com" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website</a></p>
<p><a href="https://www.quigleyforchicago.com/priorities" rel="nofollow noopener">Mike Quigley&#39;s campaign has published plans for housing, public safety, and the city budget. Tap here to read them on his site, then come back to comment.</a></p>
<h2>AI summary</h2>
<h3>The platform</h3>
<p>Only Quigley: One board elected by Chicagoans in place of the city&#39;s eight police oversight bodies. No other candidate proposes changing who oversees the police.</p>
<p>Savings first: Before any new revenue, carry out the $530 million to $1.3 billion in yearly savings a city-paid report already found.</p>
<p>Housing: 50,000 homes in four years, four-flats and backyard units legal citywide, and no new rent restrictions. Giannoulias sets the same 50,000 for 2032.</p>
<p>Pensions: Says the funds will fail unless workers pay in more or collect later. Cardenas agrees; Giannoulias and Mendoza would restructure and pay ahead.</p>
<h3>Next to the other candidates</h3>
<p>New taxes: Savings first, before any new revenue. Make the wealthy pay more: Johnson and Giannoulias. No new taxes: Kelly and Kitover. Cut taxes: Wilson.</p>
<p>Pensions: Workers share the cost, with Cardenas. Restructure the funds and pay ahead: Giannoulias and Mendoza. Change the state constitution: Wilson. Bankruptcy on the table: Kitover.</p>
<p>TIF money: Keep it in the neighborhoods it came from, with Mendoza. Send the surplus to public schools: Johnson.</p>
